  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "We note that $w=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $2w,3w,$ and $9w.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(2w,3w,9w)=18w.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $18w,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2w-3}{3w} & =\\frac{1-w}{2w}-\\frac{4w-1}{9w} \\\\ 18w\\cdot (\\frac{2w-3}{3w}) & =18w\\cdot (\\frac{1-w}{2w}-\\frac{4w-1}{9w}) \\\\ 18w\\cdot (\\frac{2w-3}{3w}) & =18w\\cdot (\\frac{1-w}{2w})+18w\\cdot (-\\frac{4w-1}{9w}) \\\\ 6\\cdot 3w\\cdot (\\frac{2w-3}{3w}) & =9\\cdot 2w\\cdot (\\frac{1-w}{2w})-2\\cdot 9w\\cdot (\\frac{4w-1}{9w}) \\\\ 6\\cdot \\cancel{3w}\\cdot (\\frac{2w-3}{\\cancel{3w}}) & =9\\cdot \\cancel{2w}\\cdot (\\frac{1-w}{\\cancel{2w}})-2\\cdot \\cancel{9w}\\cdot (\\frac{4w-1}{\\cancel{9w}}) \\\\ 6\\cdot (2w-3) & =9\\cdot (1-w)-2\\cdot (4w-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $w,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(2w-3) & =9(1-w)-2(4w-1) \\\\ 12w-18 & =9-9w-8w+2 \\\\ 12w-18 & =11-17w \\\\ 12w+17w & =11+18 \\\\ 29w & =29 \\\\ w & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $w=0$ as a solution. But since $w=1\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $w=1.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
